title:Dr. Masao Takaku
date.modified:2023-05-11
date.available:2013-08-23

[[{{image './images/201609-c4ljp.jpg' 'At Code4Lib JAPAN Conference 2016 (September, 2016)' thumbs 200px right}}|./profile-images.html]]

Dr. Masao Takaku is an Associate Professor in the Institute of Library, Information and Media Science at the University of Tsukuba. His research and teaching are in the fields of library and information science and informatics, with a particular focus on knowledge organization, digital libraries, information retrieval, scholarly communication, and digital archives. He received his Ph.D. in Informatics from the Graduate School of Library, Information and Media Studies at the University of Tsukuba in 2004. Before joining the University of Tsukuba in 2013, he worked at the National Institute of Informatics (NII), the Research Organization of Information and Systems (ROIS), and the National Institute for Materials Science (NIMS).

His main research interest is how diverse information resources can be described, organized, interlinked, and made sustainably available in ways that facilitate their discovery and use. In recent years, he has been particularly interested in building information infrastructures using technologies such as Linked Open Data (LOD), RDF, knowledge graphs, and metadata, as well as in examining how such infrastructures can be used in practice. Rather than focusing solely on data models and technical specifications, his research also addresses issues that arise through the actual construction, publication, and long-term operation of datasets and information services.

One example of this work is the development and publication of datasets for information resources used in school education, including [[Textbook LOD|https://w3id.org/jp-textbook/]] and [[Course of Study LOD|https://w3id.org/jp-cos/]]. By interlinking textbooks, teaching units, subjects and courses, and national curriculum standards, these projects aim to provide an infrastructure that enables educational information previously scattered across separate resources and databases to be searched and reused across sources. This research addresses not only data modeling, identifier design, vocabulary design, quality control using technologies such as SHACL, and documentation, but also methods for publishing data on the Web in forms that are usable by both humans and machines, as well as the design of sustainable infrastructures for long-term data publication.

In the areas of digital libraries and digital archives, his research explores the use of metadata and Linked Data for publishing cultural and research resources, the discoverability of information resources, and sustainable approaches to long-term information provision. He places particular emphasis on ensuring that data and software developed through research are not limited to academic publications, but are made openly available and maintained as working resources. This interest has also led to his continued involvement in open data and open-source software, including the development of software for data publication and Web-based dissemination.

He also conducts research on scholarly communication. His work examines persistent identifiers (PIDs), including DOIs, bibliographic and citation information, and research output metadata, with a focus on how scholarly resources are described and identified and how they are connected across different information services. By comparing scholarly information infrastructures and open research information sources in Japan and internationally, he investigates how the development and use of identifiers and metadata affect the discovery and reuse of scholarly information.

At the University of Tsukuba, he teaches courses related to knowledge organization, digital documents, and information technology, covering topics such as metadata, Linked Data, Web technologies, and information retrieval. In his teaching, he emphasizes practical engagement with information resources: not only learning established concepts and rules, but also understanding how real-world resources can be described, searched, published, interpreted, and connected with other data.

Alongside his research and teaching, he has been actively involved in library and information technology communities, including [[Code4Lib JAPAN|https://www.code4lib.jp]] and [[saveMLAK|https://savemlak.jp]]. He is particularly interested in communities where practitioners and researchers from libraries, research institutions, digital archives, and information technology can share knowledge and technical expertise. Through these activities, he seeks to bridge research and practice, as well as institutional and technological perspectives. A fundamental theme running through his research is how to create environments in which information resources are easier to discover, connect, and reuse, while remaining sustainably available over time, considering both technological and user-oriented perspectives.

His detailed profile is available at [[curriculum vitae page|./profile.html]].
